1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,056
What happens when a country's banks become

2
00:00:03,056 --> 00:00:05,762
so big that the whole country cannot

3
00:00:05,762 --> 00:00:06,903
save them?

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:09,436
In October twenty oh eight,

5
00:00:09,436 --> 00:00:11,320
Iceland found out.

6
00:00:11,490 --> 00:00:14,328
Its three biggest banks collapsed in less

7
00:00:14,328 --> 00:00:15,780
than one week.

8
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:18,967
According to the International Monetary

9
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:19,369
Fund,

10
00:00:19,369 --> 00:00:21,481
the banking system had grown to almost

11
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:23,383
nine times the size of the country's

12
00:00:23,383 --> 00:00:25,250
entire economy.

13
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:28,166
The prime minister went on television and

14
00:00:28,166 --> 00:00:30,600
warned of national bankruptcy.

15
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:32,880
And the British government froze an

16
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,169
Icelandic bank's money using an

17
00:00:35,169 --> 00:00:37,142
anti-terrorism law.

18
00:00:47,783 --> 00:00:50,145
How does a country of fishermen build

19
00:00:50,145 --> 00:00:52,399
a global banking empire?

20
00:00:56,783 --> 00:00:59,191
For most of its modern history,

21
00:00:59,191 --> 00:01:02,213
Iceland lived mainly on two things.

22
00:01:02,213 --> 00:01:02,846
Fishing,

23
00:01:02,846 --> 00:01:04,853
and later aluminium.

24
00:01:04,853 --> 00:01:06,015
Its banks were small,

25
00:01:06,015 --> 00:01:08,513
and mostly owned by the state.

26
00:01:08,753 --> 00:01:09,126
Then,

27
00:01:09,126 --> 00:01:11,069
around the turn of the century,

28
00:01:11,069 --> 00:01:13,043
the government sold them off.

29
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:16,106
A study from the University of Cambridge

30
00:01:16,106 --> 00:01:18,674
describes the sale as arbitrary and

31
00:01:18,674 --> 00:01:19,295
opaque,

32
00:01:19,295 --> 00:01:20,793
with the banks going to groups close

33
00:01:20,793 --> 00:01:22,892
to the parties in power.

34
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:25,221
From about twenty oh three,

35
00:01:25,221 --> 00:01:26,244
the three banks,

36
00:01:26,244 --> 00:01:27,004
Kaupthing,

37
00:01:27,004 --> 00:01:28,814
Landsbanki and Glitnir,

38
00:01:28,814 --> 00:01:32,560
began to grow at an extraordinary speed.

39
00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:34,871
Money was cheap around the world,

40
00:01:34,871 --> 00:01:37,187
and capital could flow across borders

41
00:01:37,187 --> 00:01:38,250
freely.

42
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:40,412
The banks borrowed abroad,

43
00:01:40,412 --> 00:01:41,720
invested abroad,

44
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:44,760
and lent to Iceland's own businessmen.

45
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:45,778
In fact,

46
00:01:45,778 --> 00:01:47,977
the owners of the banks became some

47
00:01:47,977 --> 00:01:50,287
of their biggest borrowers.

48
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:52,630
Let me draw the problem.

49
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:55,016
Here is the size of Iceland's economy,

50
00:01:55,016 --> 00:01:57,960
everything the country produces in a year.

51
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,430
One bar.

52
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:01,761
Now here are the assets of its

53
00:02:01,761 --> 00:02:04,052
banks by the end of twenty oh

54
00:02:04,052 --> 00:02:04,882
seven,

55
00:02:04,882 --> 00:02:07,200
according to the IMF.

56
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:08,650
Almost nine bars.

57
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:09,269
A small country,

58
00:02:09,269 --> 00:02:10,500
holding up a giant.

59
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,595
And if the giant ever fell,

60
00:02:12,595 --> 00:02:14,963
who could possibly catch it?

61
00:02:15,217 --> 00:02:16,374
To keep growing,

62
00:02:16,374 --> 00:02:18,567
the banks needed more money.

63
00:02:18,567 --> 00:02:20,667
So they went looking for savers.

64
00:02:20,667 --> 00:02:21,931
In Britain.

65
00:02:27,583 --> 00:02:29,790
Why would a British saver put their

66
00:02:29,790 --> 00:02:32,597
money in an Icelandic bank?

67
00:02:36,583 --> 00:02:38,743
Because it paid more.

68
00:02:38,743 --> 00:02:40,624
In October twenty oh six,

69
00:02:40,624 --> 00:02:42,949
Landsbanki launched an internet savings

70
00:02:42,949 --> 00:02:45,283
account in Britain called Icesave.

71
00:02:45,283 --> 00:02:47,959
It offered some of the best interest

72
00:02:47,959 --> 00:02:49,637
rates available,

73
00:02:49,637 --> 00:02:52,183
and the deposits flooded in.

74
00:02:52,243 --> 00:02:54,853
It also took savers in the Netherlands.

75
00:02:54,863 --> 00:02:55,736
In the end,

76
00:02:55,736 --> 00:02:57,975
around three hundred and forty thousand

77
00:02:57,975 --> 00:03:00,215
British and Dutch savers had money in

78
00:03:00,215 --> 00:03:01,039
it.

79
00:03:01,283 --> 00:03:03,533
Let me draw the catch.

80
00:03:03,703 --> 00:03:05,823
Here is a British saver.

81
00:03:06,243 --> 00:03:07,515
Here is Icesave,

82
00:03:07,515 --> 00:03:09,649
a branch of an Icelandic bank,

83
00:03:09,649 --> 00:03:11,973
not a separate British one.

84
00:03:12,193 --> 00:03:13,438
If the bank fails,

85
00:03:13,438 --> 00:03:16,742
the first promise to repay ordinary savers

86
00:03:16,742 --> 00:03:17,987
comes from here,

87
00:03:17,987 --> 00:03:21,383
Iceland's own deposit guarantee fund.

88
00:03:21,653 --> 00:03:23,790
A fund in a small country,

89
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:26,766
standing behind savers in two much bigger

90
00:03:26,766 --> 00:03:27,907
ones.

91
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:30,240
There were warning signs.

92
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,038
Iceland was running huge deficits with the

93
00:03:33,038 --> 00:03:34,099
rest of the world,

94
00:03:34,099 --> 00:03:36,481
and its banks depended on borrowing that

95
00:03:36,481 --> 00:03:38,380
could vanish overnight.

96
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:40,709
The Cambridge study notes that the banks

97
00:03:40,709 --> 00:03:42,973
did not hold the toxic mortgage-linked

98
00:03:42,973 --> 00:03:45,220
securities that sank others.

99
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:47,200
Their weakness was simpler.

100
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,305
They were too big,

101
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:50,934
and they ran on borrowed money.

102
00:03:51,183 --> 00:03:52,108
And yet,

103
00:03:52,108 --> 00:03:54,303
the experts were calm.

104
00:03:54,353 --> 00:03:55,989
In twenty oh seven,

105
00:03:55,989 --> 00:03:58,836
a report by respected international

106
00:03:58,836 --> 00:04:00,709
economists concluded,

107
00:04:00,709 --> 00:04:01,871
in its own words,

108
00:04:01,871 --> 00:04:03,769
that these were in fact very well

109
00:04:03,769 --> 00:04:04,843
run banks.

110
00:04:05,223 --> 00:04:07,733
Iceland's regulators seemed to agree.

111
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:09,664
When the people paid to watch the

112
00:04:09,664 --> 00:04:10,940
banks were reassured,

113
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:13,320
very few outsiders asked the obvious

114
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:14,353
question.

115
00:04:14,513 --> 00:04:16,925
What if the money stops?

116
00:04:17,183 --> 00:04:17,702
Then,

117
00:04:17,702 --> 00:04:20,133
in September twenty oh eight,

118
00:04:20,133 --> 00:04:24,012
the global flow of borrowed money stopped.

119
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:33,190
What happens when the money stops?

120
00:04:38,650 --> 00:04:41,204
After the collapse of Lehman Brothers,

121
00:04:41,204 --> 00:04:43,781
banks everywhere stopped lending to each

122
00:04:43,781 --> 00:04:44,850
other.

123
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:46,505
For Iceland's banks,

124
00:04:46,505 --> 00:04:48,030
that was fatal.

125
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:50,320
They could not roll over their debts,

126
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:52,485
and the country's central bank could not

127
00:04:52,485 --> 00:04:54,890
print the foreign money needed to rescue

128
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:55,730
them.

129
00:04:56,100 --> 00:04:58,149
One after another,

130
00:04:58,149 --> 00:04:59,010
Glitnir,

131
00:04:59,010 --> 00:05:01,355
Landsbanki and Kaupthing fell,

132
00:05:01,355 --> 00:05:03,440
in less than one week.

133
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:04,435
Together,

134
00:05:04,435 --> 00:05:06,735
they were about eighty-five percent of the

135
00:05:06,735 --> 00:05:08,590
country's banking system.

136
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:10,363
On October sixth,

137
00:05:10,363 --> 00:05:12,927
Prime Minister Geir Haarde addressed the

138
00:05:12,927 --> 00:05:14,040
nation.

139
00:05:14,270 --> 00:05:14,867
There was,

140
00:05:14,867 --> 00:05:15,464
he said,

141
00:05:15,464 --> 00:05:17,934
a very real danger that the economy

142
00:05:17,934 --> 00:05:19,787
could be sucked with the banks into

143
00:05:19,787 --> 00:05:20,590
the whirlpool,

144
00:05:20,590 --> 00:05:22,442
and the result could be national

145
00:05:22,442 --> 00:05:23,660
bankruptcy.

146
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:25,942
He announced an emergency bill,

147
00:05:25,942 --> 00:05:27,950
to be passed that same day.

148
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:30,280
And he ended with three words.

149
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:32,375
God bless Iceland.

150
00:05:32,617 --> 00:05:33,767
The next day,

151
00:05:33,767 --> 00:05:35,508
October seventh,

152
00:05:35,508 --> 00:05:38,997
the government took control of the banks.

153
00:05:38,997 --> 00:05:40,857
Measured together,

154
00:05:40,857 --> 00:05:43,823
the failure of the three Icelandic banks

155
00:05:43,823 --> 00:05:46,519
was the third largest bankruptcy in the

156
00:05:46,519 --> 00:05:47,032
world,

157
00:05:47,032 --> 00:05:49,728
after Lehman Brothers and Washington

158
00:05:49,728 --> 00:05:50,867
Mutual.

159
00:05:51,117 --> 00:05:52,407
Two days later,

160
00:05:52,407 --> 00:05:53,797
Britain struck.

161
00:05:53,807 --> 00:05:55,847
At ten in the morning on October

162
00:05:55,847 --> 00:05:56,234
eighth,

163
00:05:56,234 --> 00:05:58,274
the British Treasury made an order

164
00:05:58,274 --> 00:06:00,109
freezing Landsbanki's assets in the

165
00:06:00,109 --> 00:06:01,117
country.

166
00:06:01,537 --> 00:06:03,467
The power it used came from a

167
00:06:03,467 --> 00:06:04,946
law of twenty oh one,

168
00:06:04,946 --> 00:06:06,426
the anti-terrorism,

169
00:06:06,426 --> 00:06:08,527
crime and security act.

170
00:06:08,577 --> 00:06:10,243
An anti-terrorism law,

171
00:06:10,243 --> 00:06:12,352
used against the bank of a friendly

172
00:06:12,352 --> 00:06:14,007
European country.

173
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:16,957
Let me draw why a country cannot

174
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:18,760
save banks this big.

175
00:06:19,030 --> 00:06:20,467
Here is the government,

176
00:06:20,467 --> 00:06:23,150
with its tax revenue and its reserves.

177
00:06:23,170 --> 00:06:24,379
Here are the banks,

178
00:06:24,379 --> 00:06:26,460
with their debts to the world.

179
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:29,027
An arrow from the government to the

180
00:06:29,027 --> 00:06:30,550
banks is a rescue.

181
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:32,660
But look at the sizes.

182
00:06:32,710 --> 00:06:34,676
The banks owe many times more than

183
00:06:34,676 --> 00:06:37,440
the whole economy produces.

184
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,890
The arrow simply cannot reach.

185
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:42,296
So instead of saving the banks,

186
00:06:42,296 --> 00:06:44,786
Iceland drew a line between them and

187
00:06:44,786 --> 00:06:46,133
the country.

188
00:06:46,383 --> 00:06:48,433
The banks were gone.

189
00:06:48,633 --> 00:06:51,431
Now the bill arrived at every kitchen

190
00:06:51,431 --> 00:06:52,498
table.

191
00:06:58,150 --> 00:07:01,125
Who pays when a whole banking system

192
00:07:01,125 --> 00:07:02,617
disappears?

193
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:07,618
First,

194
00:07:07,618 --> 00:07:10,930
everyone holding Iceland's currency.

195
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:12,707
According to the IMF,

196
00:07:12,707 --> 00:07:15,364
the currency fell by more than seventy

197
00:07:15,364 --> 00:07:17,273
percent in offshore trading,

198
00:07:17,273 --> 00:07:19,447
and the stock market fell by eighty

199
00:07:19,447 --> 00:07:20,530
percent.

200
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:22,551
Even at official rates,

201
00:07:22,551 --> 00:07:24,483
the currency lost more than half of

202
00:07:24,483 --> 00:07:26,631
its value against the dollar in about

203
00:07:26,631 --> 00:07:27,660
a year.

204
00:07:28,030 --> 00:07:30,513
Imports became far more expensive,

205
00:07:30,513 --> 00:07:33,022
and inflation rose to over eighteen

206
00:07:33,022 --> 00:07:35,378
percent in twenty oh nine.

207
00:07:35,617 --> 00:07:38,007
Then came the recession.

208
00:07:38,287 --> 00:07:41,005
The economy shrank by minus six point

209
00:07:41,005 --> 00:07:43,204
six percent in twenty oh nine,

210
00:07:43,204 --> 00:07:45,675
and by another minus four point one

211
00:07:45,675 --> 00:07:47,757
percent the year after.

212
00:07:48,107 --> 00:07:50,400
According to the Cambridge study,

213
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:52,717
it was the worst recession of any

214
00:07:52,717 --> 00:07:54,084
country in the OECD,

215
00:07:54,084 --> 00:07:56,537
the club of rich economies.

216
00:07:56,797 --> 00:07:58,409
Unemployment soared,

217
00:07:58,409 --> 00:08:01,580
and the weak currency pushed prices up.

218
00:08:01,817 --> 00:08:02,912
In November,

219
00:08:02,912 --> 00:08:05,370
the IMF approved a loan to Iceland

220
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:07,805
of about two point one billion dollars,

221
00:08:07,805 --> 00:08:10,263
with other governments lending the rest of

222
00:08:10,263 --> 00:08:11,757
what was needed.

223
00:08:12,017 --> 00:08:12,952
In return,

224
00:08:12,952 --> 00:08:16,068
Iceland had to stabilize its currency and

225
00:08:16,068 --> 00:08:18,107
its public finances.

226
00:08:18,350 --> 00:08:20,720
And then there was Icesave.

227
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:22,800
Britain and the Netherlands paid back

228
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:23,856
their own savers,

229
00:08:23,856 --> 00:08:26,180
and sent the bill to Iceland.

230
00:08:26,310 --> 00:08:28,375
Britain alone had paid out four and

231
00:08:28,375 --> 00:08:29,728
a half billion pounds,

232
00:08:29,728 --> 00:08:32,251
about seven point eight billion dollars at

233
00:08:32,251 --> 00:08:33,310
the time.

234
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:35,959
The governments negotiated a deal for

235
00:08:35,959 --> 00:08:37,957
Iceland's taxpayers to repay it,

236
00:08:37,957 --> 00:08:39,364
with interest.

237
00:08:39,617 --> 00:08:41,997
Icelanders said no.

238
00:08:42,187 --> 00:08:44,730
In a referendum in March twenty ten,

239
00:08:44,730 --> 00:08:47,081
just over ninety-three percent voted

240
00:08:47,081 --> 00:08:49,157
against the repayment deal.

241
00:08:49,157 --> 00:08:49,816
A new,

242
00:08:49,816 --> 00:08:51,860
softer deal went to a second vote

243
00:08:51,860 --> 00:08:54,277
in April twenty eleven.

244
00:08:54,407 --> 00:08:56,136
It was rejected again,

245
00:08:56,136 --> 00:08:58,487
by almost fifty-nine percent.

246
00:08:58,637 --> 00:09:01,492
Ordinary people refused to pay for the

247
00:09:01,492 --> 00:09:03,190
debts of private banks.

248
00:09:03,450 --> 00:09:06,800
Britain and the Netherlands took the fight

249
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:08,070
to court.

250
00:09:13,717 --> 00:09:16,438
Did Iceland have to pay?

251
00:09:22,717 --> 00:09:24,765
The case went to the EFTA Court,

252
00:09:24,765 --> 00:09:26,836
the court for the European countries

253
00:09:26,836 --> 00:09:29,367
outside the European Union that share its

254
00:09:29,367 --> 00:09:30,887
single market.

255
00:09:31,087 --> 00:09:32,537
On January twenty-eighth,

256
00:09:32,537 --> 00:09:33,567
twenty thirteen,

257
00:09:33,567 --> 00:09:34,797
it ruled.

258
00:09:34,867 --> 00:09:37,427
The claim against Iceland was dismissed.

259
00:09:37,597 --> 00:09:39,879
Iceland had not broken the rules by

260
00:09:39,879 --> 00:09:42,369
refusing to guarantee the savers with its

261
00:09:42,369 --> 00:09:44,006
own taxpayers' money.

262
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:48,000
The savers' money came back another way.

263
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:50,659
Landsbanki's remaining assets were sold

264
00:09:50,659 --> 00:09:52,741
off over the following years,

265
00:09:52,741 --> 00:09:55,680
and the deposit claims had priority.

266
00:09:55,950 --> 00:09:57,948
In January twenty sixteen,

267
00:09:57,948 --> 00:10:00,418
the British Treasury announced it had

268
00:10:00,418 --> 00:10:02,417
received its final payment,

269
00:10:02,417 --> 00:10:04,662
and said it had now recovered all

270
00:10:04,662 --> 00:10:07,732
the taxpayer money used for Icesave.

271
00:10:07,983 --> 00:10:10,556
Iceland also did something few countries

272
00:10:10,556 --> 00:10:12,283
did after the crisis.

273
00:10:12,283 --> 00:10:14,283
It took people to court.

274
00:10:14,283 --> 00:10:15,475
In twenty twelve,

275
00:10:15,475 --> 00:10:17,664
former prime minister Geir Haarde was

276
00:10:17,664 --> 00:10:19,343
convicted on one charge,

277
00:10:19,343 --> 00:10:20,778
with no punishment,

278
00:10:20,778 --> 00:10:23,454
the first government leader prosecuted

279
00:10:23,454 --> 00:10:26,243
over the global banking crisis.

280
00:10:26,563 --> 00:10:27,955
In twenty fifteen,

281
00:10:27,955 --> 00:10:30,316
Iceland's Supreme Court sent the former

282
00:10:30,316 --> 00:10:32,440
head of Kaupthing to prison for five

283
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:35,872
and a half years for market manipulation.

284
00:10:36,117 --> 00:10:38,867
So what is the lesson of Iceland?

285
00:10:38,937 --> 00:10:40,722
That a bank can grow bigger than

286
00:10:40,722 --> 00:10:43,107
the country that stands behind it.

287
00:10:43,167 --> 00:10:44,283
When that happens,

288
00:10:44,283 --> 00:10:46,539
the country stops controlling the bank,

289
00:10:46,539 --> 00:10:48,361
and the bank starts controlling the

290
00:10:48,361 --> 00:10:49,417
country.

291
00:10:49,667 --> 00:10:50,566
In the end,

292
00:10:50,566 --> 00:10:52,388
Iceland let its banks fail,

293
